@meta
id: 09-17
title: Mark one end, delete to it
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-to-mark, op-linewise
par: 18
minutes: 5
vscode: click the first line, scroll, shift-click the last, Delete
open: orders.fixture.ts

@brief
Fixtures `ORD-406` through `ORD-414` belonged to the CSV importer test,
which was deleted last sprint. They are dead weight and one of them
breaks the new total assertion.

Remove those nine entries. Everything above `ORD-406` and everything from
`ORD-415` down stays.

@keys
| `ma` | drop a mark called `a` on this line |
| `'a` | jump back to the line holding mark `a` |
| `d'a` | delete every line from here to the marked line |
| `` `a `` | jump to the exact character the mark was set on |

@hints
You can see both ends of the dead run, but they are nine lines apart and
counting nine lines correctly at speed is optimistic. Put something at one
end instead.
--
`ma` marks the line under the cursor. Marks are invisible and free, and
there are twenty-six of them. Once one end is marked, go to the other end
and delete back to it.
--
`7G` onto the `ORD-406` line, `ma`, then `15G` onto the `ORD-414` line,
then `d'a`.

@solution
    7G ma 15G d'a :wq<Enter>

`'a` is a motion. That is the entire idea: once you accept that, `d'a`,
`y'a` and `c'a` need no separate explanation, and neither will every other
motion you meet for the rest of your vim career.

Marks change the shape of an edit in a way worth noticing. Every operator
so far has needed you to commit to the extent *before* you could see the
result — `d9j` means deciding the number first and finding out afterwards.
With a mark you go to one end, drop it, travel to the other end by
whatever means you like, look at where you actually are, and only then
press `d`. Nothing is destroyed until both ends are confirmed by eye.

Two flavours, and the difference matters:

    d'a     line mark — whole lines, from this line to that line
    d`a     character mark — from this character to that character

The straight quote is linewise, the backtick is charwise. The same split
runs through every one of vim's position keys, and `'` versus `` ` `` is
how you choose.

Marks survive until you set them again or close the file, so `ma` at the
top of a function you keep returning to is worth the two keystrokes.
Chapter 17 does the rest of them, including the ones vim sets for you.

